# The builtin table, as declarations.
#
# ## What this file is
#
# A mirror of `intrinsics()` in `src/check/types.dawn`, which is the truth.
# There the table is built by helper calls around loops that mint families of
# names, so reading one signature off it means running the constructors in
# your head and reading the whole set means running the file. Here it is 98
# lines, and the answer to "what can I call, and what does it take" is one
# scroll.
#
# ## Machine-maintained
#
# `scripts/builtin-decl-contract` compares the two in both directions on every
# push: a name here that is no builtin is red, a builtin missing from here is
# red, and a signature that differs by one character is red. So this cannot go
# stale, and equally it cannot be edited into something the compiler does not
# do -- when the two disagree, the table is right and this file is what
# changes.
#
# It also reads each line back with the compiler's own parser and renders the
# result again (P6). Agreeing with the table is not enough on its own: both
# sides are the same rendering, so a rendering that loses something leaves
# every other judgement green. That one found three signatures spelling an
# effect variable with nowhere it was introduced. `cast` is the single line
# not read back, for the reason the section below gives.
#
# ## How to read a line
#
#   * `pub fn` is a builtin any Dawn file may call.
#   * a bare `fn` is std-only: it is the backend contract behind a `std`
#     module, the checker hides it from every other module, and the module
#     that wraps it is named in the line above.
#   * ` # comptime: rejected` marks a builtin a `const` may not fold through
#     (`comptime_rejects` in `src/ir/interp.dawn`). 67 of the 98 carry it,
#     which is the real answer to "what can a const do" -- spec 7.2.2 answers
#     in prose and names about a quarter of them.
#   * one declaration per line, never wrapped. A signature is what is being
#     compared, and a comparison of something reflowed is a comparison of the
#     reflowing.
#
# ## Why it does not compile, and what still reads it
#
# A declaration with no body is a parse error (`src/front/parser.dawn`), so
# this file cannot be built even by accident, and it sits beside `src/` rather
# than inside it, so no build looks for it. That is the intended arrangement:
# it is a document whose accuracy happens to be gated, not a header the
# compiler reads.
#
# `dawn fmt` is the exception, and it is a real one rather than a courtesy:
# the formatter is lexical, so it reformats this file happily and the `fmt
# --check` gate covers `selfhost/`. The whitespace here is therefore the
# formatter's, not a generator's -- one blank line between sections, one space
# before a trailing comment -- and an edit that spaces the comptime marker
# differently is red twice over, once from the formatter and once from the
# contract.
#
# ## The eight lines that are not legal Dawn even with a body
#
# Measured, by giving every signature a `= todo()` body and checking it: 90 of
# the 98 are ordinary Dawn source. The exceptions are named rather than
# quietly rendered into something that parses.
#
#   * the seven naming `Array` -- the six `array_*` and `bytes_from_array`.
#     `Array` is a std-only type name (`BtStdOnly`), so those lines check
#     inside `std/` and nowhere else. That is the same fact their bare `fn`
#     already states, one level up in the type.
#   * `cast`, whose parameter is rendered `java.lang.Object`. A dotted type
#     path is not a spelling the parser takes anywhere; the source spelling is
#     `use java "java.lang.Object"` and then the bare `Object`. The fully
#     qualified name is used here because this file carries no `use` lines and
#     a bare `Object` would name nothing -- identity, at the cost of the one
#     line's paste-ability.
#
# The lowering-internal intrinsics (`internal_intrinsics()` in
# `src/ir/lower.dawn`, 19 names) are deliberately absent. No source file may
# write them under any visibility -- they exist between lowering and the
# emitters -- so a declaration would be a promise this file cannot keep.

# ---- control -- diverging, and the two barriers plus the release primitive ----
#
# The three that decide where a failure stops. `panic` starts one, the two
# `catch_*` builtins are the only places one becomes a value, and `bracket`
# releases across one without catching it (spec 9.8).

## abort with a message; it diverges, so it types wherever a value is wanted
pub fn panic(msg: String) -> Never

## a placeholder that panics when reached
pub fn todo() -> Never

## use an acquired resource and release it on every way out -- the value, a panic or a fault; the failure carries on unchanged (this catches nothing)
pub fn bracket[A, B, !e](resource: A, release: fn(A) -> Unit !e, body: fn(A) -> B !e) -> B !e # comptime: rejected

## run a closure, turning an external failure into Err (panics pass through)
pub fn catch_fault[T, !e](f: fn() -> T !e) -> Result[T, ForeignError] !e # comptime: rejected

## run a closure at an isolation boundary, catching a panic or an exception into Err
pub fn catch_panic[T, !e](f: fn() -> T !e) -> Result[T, ForeignError] !io # comptime: rejected

## abandon the computation a continuation would have resumed, running every bracket release inside it; the one-shot ticket is spent, so resuming afterwards panics
pub fn discard[T, U, !e](k: fn(T) -> U !e) -> Unit !e # comptime: rejected

# ---- option -- the two the prelude type is worth having ----

## unwrap a Some or panic with a message
pub fn expect[T](o: Option[T], msg: String) -> T

## the Some value, or a fallback for None
pub fn unwrap_or[T](o: Option[T], fallback: T) -> T

# ---- conversion -- between the scalars, and out to text ----

## Int to Float
pub fn to_float(n: Int) -> Float

## Float to Int (truncates)
pub fn to_int(x: Float) -> Int

## render any printable value (numbers, strings, derive Show data)
pub fn to_string[T: Show](x: T) -> String

# ---- list -- what the language needs before std exists ----
#
# Four, not a list API: `std/list` is the list API. These are the ones the
# language itself reaches for -- `for` and indexing lower onto them, and a
# sort needs a comparison the checker can see.

## number of elements
pub fn len[T](xs: List[T]) -> Int

## element at an index; None when out of bounds
pub fn get[T](xs: List[T], i: Int) -> Option[T]

## the integers from..to (exclusive)
pub fn range(from: Int, to: Int) -> List[Int]

## stable sort with a two-argument cmp function
pub fn sort_by[T, !e](xs: List[T], cmp: fn(T, T) -> Int !e) -> List[T] !e

# ---- string -- text in, numbers out ----
#
# `str_lower`/`str_upper` are the Unicode case table itself. std/str spells
# `to_lower`/`to_upper` over them, and two names for one operation is one too
# many, so the primitives are std-only (audit RD-02).

## concatenate with a separator
pub fn join(xs: List[String], sep: String) -> String

## parse a decimal integer; None on malformed input
pub fn parse_int(s: String) -> Option[Int]

## parse a floating point number; None on malformed input
pub fn parse_float(s: String) -> Option[Float]

## parse an integer in base 2 through 36; None on malformed input
pub fn parse_int_radix(s: String, radix: Int) -> Option[Int] # comptime: rejected

## the Unicode case table applied downwards; `str.to_lower` is the spelling user code writes
fn str_lower(s: String) -> String

## the Unicode case table applied upwards; `str.to_upper` is the spelling user code writes
fn str_upper(s: String) -> String

# ---- char -- Unicode scalar values, and the six classes ----
#
# `Char` is an opaque `Int`. `char_unchecked` is the bridge back, std-only for
# the reason an unchecked reader always is.

## split a string into Unicode scalar-value characters
pub fn code_points(s: String) -> List[Char]

## assemble a string from characters
pub fn from_code_points(cs: List[Char]) -> String

## whether a character is a Unicode letter
pub fn char_is_letter(c: Char) -> Bool

## whether a character is a decimal digit
pub fn char_is_digit(c: Char) -> Bool

## whether a character is a letter or decimal digit
pub fn char_is_alnum(c: Char) -> Bool

## whether a character is upper-case
pub fn char_is_upper(c: Char) -> Bool

## whether a character is lower-case
pub fn char_is_lower(c: Char) -> Bool

## whether a character is whitespace
pub fn char_is_space(c: Char) -> Bool

## an Int taken as a Char with no range check; lowering rewrites the call to its argument
fn char_unchecked(n: Int) -> Char # comptime: rejected

# ---- cursor -- the string walk std/str is written over ----
#
# A cursor is a byte offset into a string, and this family is what makes
# walking one a language primitive rather than a representation user code can
# see. All std-only: `std/str` is the public surface.

## a cursor at the first character of a string
fn cursor_start(s: String) -> Int

## a cursor one past the last character
fn cursor_end(s: String) -> Int

## whether a cursor has reached the end
fn cursor_done(s: String, c: Int) -> Bool

## the code point a cursor is on
fn cursor_char(s: String, c: Int) -> Int

## the cursor one character on
fn cursor_next(s: String, c: Int) -> Int

## the cursor one character back
fn cursor_prev(s: String, c: Int) -> Int

## the substring between two cursors; the emitter writes it inline so it can check the range
fn cursor_slice(s: String, from: Int, to: Int) -> String

# ---- bytes -- the byte surface behind std/bytes ----
#
# All std-only, and the shapes say why: `bytes_at` answers -1 out of range,
# and turning that sentinel into a panic is the whole of what `bytes.at` does.

## a string's UTF-8 encoding
fn bytes_utf8(s: String) -> Bytes # comptime: rejected

## bytes read back as UTF-8
fn bytes_decode_utf8(b: Bytes) -> String # comptime: rejected

## bytes read back as Latin-1, one byte to one character
fn bytes_decode_latin1(b: Bytes) -> String # comptime: rejected

## number of bytes
fn bytes_len(b: Bytes) -> Int # comptime: rejected

## the byte at an index, or -1 out of range; `bytes.at` is what turns that into a panic
fn bytes_at(b: Bytes, i: Int) -> Int # comptime: rejected

## the bytes in a half-open range
fn bytes_slice(b: Bytes, start: Int, end: Int) -> Bytes # comptime: rejected

## bytes built from an array of byte values
fn bytes_from_array(a: Array[Int]) -> Bytes # comptime: rejected

# ---- map -- the persistent trie behind std/map ----
#
# Std-only, and erased on both sides: lowering turns each of these into a call
# on the Dawn HAMT in `std/hamt`, so a backend implements `Array` and nothing
# else.

## the empty map
fn map_empty[K, V]() -> Map[K, V] # comptime: rejected

## a map built from a list of key-value pairs
fn map_from[K: Eq + Hash, V](entries: List[(K, V)]) -> Map[K, V] # comptime: rejected

## the map with one key bound, replacing whatever it was bound to
fn map_insert[K: Eq + Hash, V](m: Map[K, V], key: K, value: V) -> Map[K, V] # comptime: rejected

## the map without one key
fn map_remove[K: Eq + Hash, V](m: Map[K, V], key: K) -> Map[K, V] # comptime: rejected

## what a key is bound to, if anything
fn map_get[K: Eq + Hash, V](m: Map[K, V], key: K) -> Option[V] # comptime: rejected

## whether a key is bound
fn map_has[K: Eq + Hash, V](m: Map[K, V], key: K) -> Bool # comptime: rejected

## number of bindings
fn map_size[K, V](m: Map[K, V]) -> Int # comptime: rejected

## the bound keys
fn map_keys[K, V](m: Map[K, V]) -> List[K] # comptime: rejected

## what the keys are bound to
fn map_values[K, V](m: Map[K, V]) -> List[V] # comptime: rejected

## the bindings, as key-value pairs
fn map_entries[K, V](m: Map[K, V]) -> List[(K, V)] # comptime: rejected

## reduce over the bindings with an accumulator
fn map_fold[K, V, A, !e](m: Map[K, V], init: A, f: fn(A, K, V) -> A !e) -> A !e # comptime: rejected

# ---- set -- the same trie, keyed on its elements ----

## the empty set
fn set_empty[T]() -> Set[T] # comptime: rejected

## a set of a list's elements
fn set_from[T: Eq + Hash](xs: List[T]) -> Set[T] # comptime: rejected

## the set with one element added
fn set_insert[T: Eq + Hash](s: Set[T], x: T) -> Set[T] # comptime: rejected

## the set without one element
fn set_remove[T: Eq + Hash](s: Set[T], x: T) -> Set[T] # comptime: rejected

## whether an element is in the set
fn set_has[T: Eq + Hash](s: Set[T], x: T) -> Bool # comptime: rejected

## number of elements
fn set_size[T](s: Set[T]) -> Int # comptime: rejected

## the elements, as a list
fn set_to_list[T](s: Set[T]) -> List[T] # comptime: rejected

# ---- array -- the one collection primitive a backend implements ----
#
# Everything else in this file that holds several values is written in Dawn
# over these six. `popcount` is here rather than with the arithmetic because
# it is the HAMT's bitmap operation and nothing else uses it.

## a new empty array
fn array_new[T]() -> Array[T] # comptime: rejected

## number of slots
fn array_len[T](a: Array[T]) -> Int # comptime: rejected

## the element in a slot
fn array_get[T](a: Array[T], i: Int) -> T # comptime: rejected

## the array with one element appended
fn array_push[T](a: Array[T], x: T) -> Array[T] # comptime: rejected

## the array with one slot overwritten; the array goes in owned, so a unique one is written in place
fn array_with[T](a: Array[T], i: Int, x: T) -> Array[T] # comptime: rejected

## array_get for a caller about to overwrite the slot: a unique array transfers the slot's own reference out
fn array_steal[T](a: Array[T], i: Int) -> T # comptime: rejected

## the number of set bits in an Int
fn popcount(n: Int) -> Int # comptime: rejected

# ---- io -- the console, the filesystem and the process ----
#
# `args` is the only public one. Every `io_*` primitive is std-only, and that
# is the error model's boundary rather than a matter of taste: `io_read_file`
# raises a *fault* on failure, and `std/io` is where that becomes a
# `Result`. Called directly, the fault reaches the caller as a JVM stack trace
# on one backend and a `longjmp` on the other.

## command line arguments
pub fn args() -> List[String] !io # comptime: rejected

## write a string to stdout
fn io_print(s: String) -> Unit !io # comptime: rejected

## write a string to stdout, followed by a newline
fn io_println(s: String) -> Unit !io # comptime: rejected

## write a string to stderr
fn io_eprint(s: String) -> Unit !io # comptime: rejected

## write a string to stderr, followed by a newline
fn io_eprintln(s: String) -> Unit !io # comptime: rejected

## one line of stdin, or None at end of input
fn io_read_line() -> Option[String] !io # comptime: rejected

## whether this process or wasm instance has an installed reactor root
fn reactor_state_has() -> Bool !io # comptime: rejected

## the installed reactor root, erased behind std/reactor's one private type
fn reactor_state_get[T]() -> T !io # comptime: rejected

## replace the installed reactor root
fn reactor_state_set[T](state: T) -> Unit !io # comptime: rejected

## exactly n bytes of stdin, shorter only at end of input
fn io_read_stdin(n: Int) -> Bytes !io # comptime: rejected

## whether stdin has a byte to read, waiting up to timeout_ms for one
fn io_stdin_ready(timeout_ms: Int) -> Bool !io # comptime: rejected

## whether anything is at a path
fn io_exists(path: String) -> Bool !io # comptime: rejected

## whether a path names a directory
fn io_is_dir(path: String) -> Bool !io # comptime: rejected

## whether a path names a symbolic link, without following it
fn io_is_symlink(path: String) -> Bool !io # comptime: rejected

## a whole file, as a UTF-8 string
fn io_read_file(path: String) -> String !io # comptime: rejected

## write a string to a file, creating missing parent directories
fn io_write_file(path: String, content: String) -> Unit !io # comptime: rejected

## a whole file, as bytes
fn io_read_bytes(path: String) -> Bytes !io # comptime: rejected

## write bytes to a file, creating missing parent directories
fn io_write_bytes(path: String, content: Bytes) -> Unit !io # comptime: rejected

## the entry names directly under a directory, in the host's order
fn io_list_names(path: String) -> List[String] !io # comptime: rejected

## create a directory and every missing directory above it
fn io_mkdirs(path: String) -> Unit !io # comptime: rejected

## remove a file or an empty directory; whether there was one to remove
fn io_delete(path: String) -> Bool !io # comptime: rejected

## move a path onto another, replacing it
fn io_rename(src: String, dst: String) -> Unit !io # comptime: rejected

## give one path the permissions another has
fn io_copy_permissions(src: String, dst: String) -> Unit !io # comptime: rejected

## the process's working directory
fn io_cwd() -> String !io # comptime: rejected

## an environment variable, if it is set
fn io_getenv(name: String) -> Option[String] !io # comptime: rejected

## a fresh directory under parent, named after prefix
fn io_temp_dir(parent: String, prefix: String) -> String !io # comptime: rejected

## a fresh empty file under parent, named after prefix, created by the host in one step
fn io_temp_file(parent: String, prefix: String) -> String !io # comptime: rejected

## end the process with a status code
fn io_exit(code: Int) -> Unit !io # comptime: rejected

## run argv[0] from PATH with argv as its arguments, redirecting the two output streams to two paths, and wait for its exit status
fn io_run(argv: List[String], out_path: String, err_path: String) -> Int !io # comptime: rejected

# ---- interop -- the one escape from the type system ----

## reclaim an erased Java Object as a concrete reference type T, taken from the expected type at the call site (interop escape; a runtime CHECKCAST guards it, and a miss is an Err rather than a throw)
pub fn cast[T](x: java.lang.Object) -> Result[T, ForeignError] # comptime: rejected
